      "decision": "REJECT",
      "reason": "Topic words match, but the abstract is too summarization-oriented and provides too little evidence about how action items are defined, localized, distinguished from non-obligatory mentions, or decomposed into arguments. Its action-item contribution is also covered more specifically by the admitted email action-detection papers.",
      "confidence": 0.94
    },
    {
      "paper_id": "manual-d19b0f6f1e",
      "decision": "ADMIT",
      "reason": "D02. The abstract provides utterance-level definitions of requests and commitments in workplace email, two annotation experiments on Enron mail, and explicit responsibility distinctions between sender and recipients. This is direct empirical evidence for request-versus-commitment classification at a finer level than whole-message genre labels.",
      "stream_id": "D02",
      "role": "DIRECT",
      "confidence": 0.99
    },
    {
      "paper_id": "doi-10-3115-1699571-1699632",
      "decision": "REJECT",
      "reason": "Topic words match only indirectly. Functional-zone segmentation can be useful preprocessing for separating quoted text, signatures, and current-message content, but the paper does not itself detect action-bearing acts, classify requests or commitments, extract action arguments, or attach deadlines. It therefore does not materially strengthen an authorized Topic 05 stream.",
      "confidence": 0.98
    },
    {
      "paper_id": "doi-10-1109-hicss-2006-528",
      "decision": "ADMIT",
      "reason": "D02. The abstract defines email-oriented speech acts and applies them to communicative-purpose and genre categorization. It is weaker than proposition-level extraction, but it materially strengthens the direct email speech-act taxonomy and baseline evidence needed to distinguish request, commitment, and related act classes.",
      "stream_id": "D02",
      "role": "DIRECT",
      "confidence": 0.93
    },
    {
      "paper_id": "doi-10-1111-j-1467-8640-1992-tb00380-x",
      "decision": "REJECT",
      "reason": "The paper is relevant conceptual background for task-oriented conversation acts, but the abstract emphasizes extending the speech-act inventory and discussing computational recognition rather than presenting sufficiently specific empirical act-classification, action-argument, or workplace-action evidence. Stronger empirical T03 evidence is available in the admitted dialogue-act modeling paper.",
      "confidence": 0.91
    },
    {
      "paper_id": "manual-1e8332286e",
      "decision": "ADMIT",
      "reason": "T08. TimeML explicitly represents events, temporal expressions, and links between events and times. That relation layer materially strengthens the representation needed to attach a temporal expression to the correct action instead of treating every nearby date as that action's deadline.",
      "stream_id": "T08",
      "role": "TRANSFER",
      "confidence": 0.98
    },
    {
      "paper_id": "manual-01c535c108",
      "decision": "ADMIT",
      "reason": "T03. The paper empirically models dialogue-act tagging with lexical, prosodic, and discourse-sequence information, directly demonstrating how neighboring conversational acts provide context for act recognition. That is useful transfer evidence for context-dependent request, agreement, and action-oriented act classification.",
      "stream_id": "T03",
      "role": "TRANSFER",
      "confidence": 0.97
    },
    {
      "paper_id": "doi-10-1145-1076034-1076094",
      "decision": "ADMIT",
      "reason": "D02. The paper directly classifies email speech acts including requests and commitments and exploits dependencies between messages in the same thread. It materially strengthens direct evidence that act classification can require conversational context rather than independent whole-message classification alone.",
      "stream_id": "D02",
      "role": "DIRECT",
      "confidence": 0.98
    },
    {
      "paper_id": "doi-10-1145-1076034-1076140",
      "decision": "ADMIT",
      "reason": "D01. The abstract specifically distinguishes whole-email action classification from localized detection and highlights the text span expressing the request for action or response. That source-grounded localization is directly aligned with the requirement to retain exact action evidence rather than merely flag an email.",
      "stream_id": "D01",
      "role": "DIRECT",
      "confidence": 0.99
    },
    {
      "paper_id": "manual-73870f8488",
      "decision": "REJECT",
      "reason": "Out of scope for this empirical reading round. It is foundational speech-act theory covering requests, orders, promises, and assertions, but the abstract provides no corpus, annotation results, computational extraction method, or workplace-communication evaluation.",
      "confidence": 0.99
    },
    {
      "paper_id": "doi-10-18653-v1-2023-acl-long-536",
      "decision": "REJECT",
      "reason": "The paper concerns general event temporal-relation extraction and a structured start/end-point representation. That is potentially useful temporal reasoning, but the abstract does not show deadline recognition, due-time or trigger semantics, or attachment of temporal expressions to requested or committed workplace actions. TimeML provides the more directly relevant event-time-linking evidence for this round.",
      "confidence": 0.94
    },
    {
      "paper_id": "doi-10-3115-1564535-1564541",
      "decision": "REJECT",
      "reason": "The abstract is relevant to D02 but mainly contributes feature selection, preprocessing, and n-gram sequence improvements for email-act classification. The central capability is already covered by admitted direct email speech-act papers that provide stronger taxonomy, request/commitment definitions, annotation evidence, or thread-context modeling, so this would consume a reading slot for largely incremental classifier evidence.",
      "confidence": 0.93
    },
    {
      "paper_id": "2106.08037",
      "decision": "ADMIT",
      "reason": "T07. The VERIFIED paper models modality as a relation between modal expressions and the specific events they modify rather than merely detecting modal cues. That event-scoped representation materially strengthens the ability to distinguish actual commitments or requested actions from possible, hypothetical, qualified, or otherwise modalized events.",
      "stream_id": "T07",
      "role": "TRANSFER",
      "confidence": 0.99
    }
  ],
  "notes": "Admission is based on the supplied abstracts and the Topic 05 project boundary. UNCHECKED identity was not treated as evidence that a paper is invalid; it affected selection only where otherwise comparable candidates overlapped, most explicitly for the rejected 2006 audio-only meeting detector versus the VERIFIED 2023 meeting action-item detector. No candidate was admitted for D04, D05, T04, T05, or T06 because the supplied abstracts did not show sufficiently direct capability for those streams."
